# Redux and RTK Query

- Use RTK Query as the default for server state: fetching, caching, invalidation, polling, and loading/error flags.
- Define feature-specific endpoints close to the owning feature when practical, and keep only shared store/bootstrap code in `src/store`.
- Define endpoints with explicit request/response types and transform responses when UI models differ from API shapes.
- Use tag-based invalidation to refresh dependent dashboard views after mutations.
- Keep Redux slices focused on client/UI state only (filters, view modes, local preferences), not duplicated server data.
- Prefer one shared base API/client configuration for the app; do not introduce parallel API layers with different auth or base URL behavior unless explicitly required.
- Compose selectors for derived state; avoid recomputing expensive transformations in components.
- Prefer generated RTK Query hooks in components; avoid manual dispatch patterns for routine API calls.
